    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran’s petrochemical industries transform hydrocarbon feedstocks into chemical building blocks and materials for further manufacturing. Their performance depends on dependable feedstock and utilities, while published capacity totals require careful separation from actual output and marketable products. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Petrochemicals", "Feedstocks", "Ethylene", "Natural gas", "Industrial capacity"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T11:47:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T11:47:54Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Petrochemicals in Iran: Feedstocks and Production | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s petrochemical production chains, the difference between capacity and output, and the limits of recent production statistics."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "feedstocks",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From hydrocarbons to manufacturing inputs",
        "content": "Petrochemicals connect Iran’s hydrocarbon resources with chemical and materials manufacturing. The crucial distinction is between using a hydrocarbon as fuel and using it as feedstock: a raw material transformed into another product. The U.S. Energy Information Administration identifies petroleum products and natural gas as feedstocks used by Iran’s petrochemical production. [s1]\n\nAn individual plant makes the relationship easier to understand. Arya Sasol’s technical catalogue describes an ethane cracker producing ethylene, a chemical intermediate supplied to its polyethylene units and other destinations. Ethylene and polyethylene are therefore different stages of a connected chain. Adding their reported production together would not automatically measure material available for final sale. The catalogue documents the production arrangement, but does not establish current throughput. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "ethane-chain",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "An illustrative ethane-to-polyethylene chain",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Ethane feedstock\"] -->|\"Supplies raw material\"| B[\"Ethane cracker\"]\n  B -->|\"Produces intermediate\"| C[\"Ethylene\"]\n  C -->|\"Feeds downstream units\"| D[\"Polyethylene production\"]\n  C -->|\"Supplies other destinations\"| E[\"Other chemical users or exports\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows describe material movements documented in the Arya Sasol catalogue. They do not represent ownership, measured flows, or verified operating conditions at the editorial cutoff.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "capacity-output",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Capacity is not output",
        "content": "Nameplate capacity describes what installed equipment is designed to produce under specified conditions. Actual production depends on whether the equipment receives feedstock and can operate. S&P Global’s 2025 reporting, citing Iran’s petrochemical leadership, identifies feedstock shortages as a reason for unused capacity. Its account also links expansion ambitions to substantial financing requirements. Announced projects should consequently be distinguished from completed and operating facilities. [s3]\n\nIran Open Data’s August 2026 analysis reports lower production in Solar Hijri year 1403 than in 1402, even as installed capacity increased. It also distinguishes total production from marketable products. These are materially different concepts: gross industrial throughput and products available for sale should not be silently substituted for one another. The short dataset below preserves that analysis’s rounded production figures as attributed estimates rather than treating them as a reconciled official series. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reported-output",
        "type": "table",
        "content": {
          "title": "Two rounded annual production estimates reported by Iran Open Data",
          "dataset_id": "iod-petrochemical-output"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "constraints-and-cutoff",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Energy dependence and recent disruption",
        "content": "Gas availability is a system-wide issue. The EIA’s 2024 assessment describes seasonal demand and insufficient storage, helping explain why abundant underground resources do not guarantee uninterrupted industrial deliveries. Feedstock and energy supply must be considered alongside installed machinery when assessing production potential. [s1]\n\nThe operating picture also changed during 2026. Associated Press reported attacks on the South Pars-associated petrochemical area in April. That reporting establishes a serious disruption context; it does not provide a verified plant-by-plant recovery inventory for October. Claims about the proportion of capacity disabled should not be adopted from belligerents’ statements without independent verification. [s5]\n\nIran’s petrochemical sector should therefore be evaluated through separate questions about feedstock security, usable capacity, actual output, and downstream demand. A current assessment additionally requires verified evidence of damage, repairs, and resumed operations. This page retains the documented industrial relationships while leaving that operational assessment open. [s1][s3][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s5"]
      }
    ],
