    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Polymers connect Iran’s chemical production with manufactured materials used in packaging and other applications. Polyethylene provides a documented example of the chain from a chemical intermediate to differentiated material grades, while recycling introduces separate questions about sorting, contamination, and product quality. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Polymers", "Polyethylene", "Plastics", "Materials manufacturing", "Recycling"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T11:47:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T11:47:54Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Polymers in Iran: Materials, Grades and Recycling | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s polymer value chain through polyethylene, material grades, manufacturing applications, and the practical limits of recycling."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "material-families",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From chemical building blocks to materials",
        "content": "Polyethylene offers an accessible entry into Iran’s polymer industries. It is a thermoplastic made by polymerizing ethylene: joining small ethylene molecules into much larger molecular chains. Iranian supplier Aras Petrochemical’s product description identifies high-density and low-density forms and applications spanning packaging, agriculture, construction, and other industries. That description explains the material family; it does not establish national production volumes or the suitability of every grade for every application. [s1]\n\nThe production chain is visible in Arya Sasol’s technical catalogue. Its documented configuration includes an ethane cracker, ethylene production, and downstream polyethylene units at Assaluyeh. This distinguishes the chemical intermediate from the polymer material. A tonne of intermediate production and a tonne of polymer production represent different stages, so totals require an explicit accounting boundary. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "grades-and-applications",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why the grade matters",
        "content": "A polymer name is not a complete product specification. Arya Sasol’s catalogue separates polyethylene grades by properties and intended applications. Its examples include film grades and materials for blow-moulded bottles and containers. A converter—the manufacturer turning polymer into a shaped product—therefore needs more information than the broad label polyethylene. [s2]\n\nThis distinction helps explain the relationship between basic materials and finished goods. Packaging is not a single application: a flexible film and a rigid bottle impose different processing and performance requirements. The catalogue documents this differentiation, but its historical product descriptions should not be read as current certification or a guarantee that a particular material is suitable for food, medical, or hazardous-goods contact. Those uses require application-specific evidence beyond this overview. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "recycling",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recycling is another production chain",
        "content": "Recycling requires its own distinctions. Basel Convention technical guidelines describe mechanical recycling through operations such as sorting, size reduction, cleaning, drying, extrusion, and pelletizing. They also distinguish solvent-based purification from chemical recycling, which breaks polymer molecules into smaller components. These are general technical categories, not evidence that a particular process operates commercially in Iran. [s3]\n\nThe U.S. Environmental Protection Agency likewise identifies variability in waste plastics as a challenge for consistent recycled-material properties. Its discussion separates conversion, depolymerization, and purification technologies, whose outputs can include polymers, chemical feedstocks, or fuels. An announced recycling project therefore needs a precise description of its input, process, and output before its contribution to new polymer manufacture can be assessed. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evaluating-sector",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a useful sector assessment should measure",
        "content": "For Iran, the documented polyethylene chain is a starting point rather than a complete polymer inventory. A fuller assessment must distinguish polymer families, grades, actual resin production, conversion into goods, and recovered material. Technical catalogues explain product differentiation; international recycling guidance explains why recovered plastics need separate quality and process descriptions. Neither source supplies a current national material balance. [s2][s3][s4]\n\nThe practical lesson is to follow the material through each stage. Chemical production, polymer manufacture, conversion, and recycling answer different questions. Keeping those stages separate makes industrial claims easier to evaluate without assuming that production capacity, useful finished products, and recovered material are equivalent measures. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
